rcent  but why will they believe that when  inflation is now close to double-digit  level  that's the challenge i think and and  that's a risk  do you include the bank of england in  that i think all major central banks  have fallen prey to the same  misconception  that just by stating an inflation target  you build into your models and your  forecasts the assumption that inflation  in the long run will always come down to  two percent  i think that's a real stretch  but the you know the bank of england was  was one of the central banks which did  do more quantitative easing more kind of  creation of money in the wake of covets  so  presumably this criticism goes to them  as well as the fans it applies to the  bank of england it applies to the  federal reserve particularly uh and it  appears they just shouldn't have been  printing that extra money they shouldn't  have been printing the extra money what  governments were doing  was enough  to deal with the consequences of cobit  and that your point is that has  contributed to what we're now seeing in  terms of the cost of living yes it and  it's true you can see it with all  central banks that they're now worried  about inflation when they weren't before  and it's not all the result of the  russian invasion of ukraine this was  foreseeable you think yes it was  foreseeable because  it was a mistaken diagnosis of what  needed to be done with the pandemic  in march 2020 people argued well there's  some disruption in financial markets we  better intervene and print money to  prevent you know a collapse of certain  financial markets  but that went away within a matter of  weeks  and at that point you would have  expected central banks to withdraw the  support that they were giving rather  than withdrawing it they doubled down  and did a lot more and i think if you  just print a great deal of money  it was most extreme in the united states  where the federal reserve  printed money at a rate  that was faster than  at any point since the second world war  after covert  after covid started 2020 2021 you know  their their money supply held by  businesses and households  was growing at 25 percent a year now  you don't have to believe in the  mechanical link between that and  inflation  but you should be asking yourself the  question yeah what do we think is going  on here you know if we keep printing  money at this rate what will happen and  the answer is obvious you'll get  inflation  but people have stopped thinking about  it in those simple terms  and they've become obsessed i think with  complex models  which  can be helpful in some circumstances but  it's not a description of how the world  works  and i think just asking the simple  question what is going on here  is a very important one to ask because  the pandemic was not like the sort of  business cycles we'd seen in the past  where people lose confidence stop  investing and spending you then cut  interest rates to stimulate demand to  get back to where the economy can  produce  that wasn't the position at all in the  pandemic  the governments and households basically  decided we'll be going to stop  functioning for a while  and  once supply goes down  the worst thing you can do at that point  is to increase demand because there's a  real imbalance between the two and if  you stimulate the money demand at the  same time as output is contracting  the result is going to be inflation now  the good news here  is that they're not printing money at  that rate anymore that's stopped so we  can expect inflation to come back down  the real question i think is how deep  will the recession have to be and there  will have to be a resistance i think  it's well who knows we may get lucky we  may get lucky maybe there's a solution  to the russian invasion of ukraine the  future is unpredictable  but  if inflation doesn't come back down as  quickly as central banks hope  and in particular doesn't come back all  the way to two percent but only to say  four  then they'll have to tighten policy  further and the particular worry at  present of course is that  the labor market in britain is very very  tight  unemployment lowest level for you know  50 years  more vacancies than there are unemployed  people for the first time ever which is  all good news  it's good it's good news but what it  means is you can't expect the supply  side of the economy is being really  constrained  and  this is you know with that tighter labor  market  with wages rising you know anywhere  between five and seven percent a year  with inflation nine percent a year and  predicted to go higher  interest rates of just one percent  don't look as if it's going to be the  vehicle for bringing inflation  right back down from double digit levels  to two percent  but at the moment markets are kind of  you know expecting what is it maybe two  two and a half percent in the uk is is  your point that that to you doesn't look  like enough well we just don't know and  i think it's a mistake to predict where  interest rates will go that's one of the  mistakes central banks have made in the  last 10 years which is to try and tell  us where interest rates will go  they don't know  and nor do we  so we have to be prepared for higher  interest rates  it's just that if you want to give a  very strong and clear statement that we  are determined to bring inflation back  to the two percent target  you need to do a bit more interest rates  now to give a very clear signal that  that's what you're prepared to do  i don't think you need to say where  rates will go because i don't think you  can know but you've got to signal that  you're prepared to take whatever action  is necessary so what would you be doing  if you were in the room well i i don't  give advice to my successes and i'm not  going to do that but nonetheless i mean  it for a very  respected former central banker to be  making these points  it is quite it's quite a serious point  you're making about policy you know here  in the uk and elsewhere as well and  there's a similar debate in the united  states where the federal reserve has  come out a great deal of criticism  for saying well we'll raise rates  now they've changed their tune they were  going to raise rates by 25 basis points  you know each meeting  that could have left them still behind  the curve a year from now  now they may well make 250 basis points  increases in successive meetings  my guess is that that's the minimum  that's going to be needed to demonstrate  strongly that you are really determined  to bring inflation down irrespective of  what happens because the big lesson from  the 1970s was that the two central banks  that were the most determined to bring  inflation back down in the 70s and took  strong action to do so  the bundesbank in germany and the swiss  national bank  not only were they more successful in  bringing inflation down they actually  had the  lowest recessions  by tackling inflation early  they prevented the need to keep raising  rates in order to create a deeper  recession  to stop an ingrained wage price spiral  pushing inflation up  and that's what's most important we've  been through you know 30 years where  people have got used to low inflation  they've accepted it they stopped talking  about inflation  unless we get back to that as quickly as  possible the risk is that people do  start to worry about inflation all the  time  and then wages are being pushed up  you get more industrial unrest  and you really need a deep recession in  order to bring inflation back down let's  hope we don't need to do that really is  the 1970s that would be the 1970s now  we're not there yet but it will need a  very clear statement and signal  by central banks to make sure that we  don't end up there  one of the things that you're kind of  pointing towards is that you know a lot  of this stuff was there in the can  before  the russian invasion of ukraine  and actually it's true i mean energy  prices these price cap increases  happened before that even though a lot  of people are attributing it to that so  it's your point that part of the energy  rise in energy costs which is kind of  the big  force of momentum here that this was  partly caused by too much qe too much  momentum in the system so i think it's  very hard to work out how much was due  to  that mistake in monetary policy and how  much was the result of you know specific  factors but what we're seeing now is  inflation is spreading across the  economy it isn't just in one sector  and that's got to be the the issue that  the bank worries about  stopping it spreading to wage increases  and to the service industries and so on  we can see in the united states which is  probably a bit ahead of us in this  respect  that their problem now is that inflation  has spread  well into the service sector which is  the biggest part of the economy  you know at the beginning people were  talking about you know second-hand car  prices etc it's all one-off effects  whenever inflation rises you can always  say oh it's this this price increase or  that price increase  no one  sits around and all prices go up at the  same time you can always point to  particular price increases  the issue really is what's the  fundamental force driving higher  inflation  and i think we've been through a period  in which interest rates have been  unnaturally low the idea that one  percent interest rates even two percent  interest rates  should be the norm i think is is a very  odd idea  to hold  do you have a kind of vague idea of what  the norm is in you know i'm not asking  for a prediction but i'm asking for your  sense as to whether that has shifted  from  you know five percent or so when  back when you were governor to something  else now  i think it's very hard to judge and i  think we'd like to get back to it but  not two percent two percent it's time i  actually think it's too low and i think  the  it's weak economic growth that has led  people to  believe that the normal level of  interest rates would be a lot lower and  i think that once we can  get to a point where  we restructure a lot of the debts of  businesses that have been able to keep  going because they could service their  debts at  almost negligible interest rates  then we can shift resources from those  companies to more productive companies  more productive sectors in the economy  productivity growth will pick up  and the natural consequence of that is  that interest rates the normal level of  interest rates will itself go back to  the sort of level we saw before okay so  yeah it's not it's not kind of you know  two percent is the norm forever i mean  i mean one of the interesting things is  that whether it's interest rates or  whether it's  the level of unemployment  people have an idea of what the normal  level is because that's what we've  experienced and then it turns out  actually we go through a period when it  looks very different  and everyone says oh now he's got a new  normal level but it's always changing  it's always changing it's just it's very  hard to see  how a market economy can function  with close to zero interest rates where  you're supposed to use the interest rate  to decide which investment project to  invest in  where the interest rate needs to be  positive to give an incentive to save to  finance that investment  to generate economic growth  interest rates need to be positive and  positive  having adjusted for inflation  well at presently they're obviously  pretty much negative adjusted for  inflation when when you you've talked  about a kind of error of policy  but has there also been an error i mean  i assume the answer is has there also  been an error  of forecasting because you know the bank  was forecasting basically flat inflation  about a year and a bit ago  and so what what's gone wrong there  well a mistake is to believe that you  can make accurate forecasts i mean  economists on the 1st of january each  year tend to make forecasts they're  always wrong  but there are good reasons why  stuff happens  first of january 2020  all the economic forecasts published  then you could have torn up a few weeks  later with the pandemic  2021  at the beginning of the year people were  trying to forecast how quickly the  economy would come back we had a second  wave forecast again didn't make much  sense  first of january 2022 no one forecast  the russian invasion of ukraine they  didn't build it into their economic  forecast so these forecasts are always  going to be wrong the more important  thing to do is not to pretend  that we know that inflation is going to  be 3.2 percent in a particular year but  we are trying to identify the risks what  are the risks on the upside and the  downside  what can we actions can we take  to mitigate those risks that's what  policy is really all about and if you  think about the risks today  the risks are firmly i think on the side  of inflation becoming more ingrained  into the system because we keep talking  about it we expect it to happen  and we adjust our wage demands and our  price  setting decisions accordingly and it's  very important to try and get back to a  world in which we  thankfully stop talking about inflation  once more  um is it is it significant or relevant  that that  that this has begun with energy i mean  does energy play a particular role a lot  of economists kind of  almost  try not to think about it just treat it  as an input but is there a significance  to the fact that there's you know we've  got very high energy costs and  does that make it kind of somewhat  different somewhat faster to previous  recessions well i think there are two  ways in which it is different  the first one is that it clearly impacts  different people in different ways  and it hurts particularly  people on low incomes  because you cannot change  your heating system you can't just say  oh gas has gone up in price maybe i'll  switch to it you know heat pump or  something you can't do that at all  quickly so you're stuck with it  no real action and these terrible  stories of people just switching off the  heating  illustrate the only course of action  they can take  so it affects the welfare of people on  low incomes  significantly  um  i think the second is  is a wider reflection of the same  problem  but the real issue here is that  we don't produce  all the energy ourselves  so we have to import quite a lot  and that means that when energy prices  go up it's a bit like the rest of the  world imposing a tax on us  as a country so we're as a country we're  worse off and there's nothing we can do  about that  that makes it much much harder  to provide help and support for those  who are hardest hit  because it's going to double down on the  hit on people  on average incomes or on better off  incomes and they just aren't enough very  rich people to tap  to trump make the transfer to keep the  rest of us  from suffering too much everyone  basically takes a hit when energy prices  go up and we can talk about how to  make it you know to help people on low  incomes but the idea that there is any  way which as a country we can avoid this  problem  isn't true when it comes to energy it  sounds like a particularly  toxic cocktail really well which is why  i said before that  when you get an intellectual mistake  in policy  and you allow inflation to start to rise  if you're then hit by bad luck  which is what happened in the 1970s and  has happened now  it becomes something of a  very unpleasant outcome  and it takes tough action and it's not a  pleasant period through which we're  going to have to go the failure of  economics is it or failure of  conventional yeah i think it i mean i  would not blame individuals in central  banks i think it's a failure of  the economics profession  to to believe that not the first one it  must be said in recent decades well  certainly in the 70s we had a similar  problem  now we've got the issue in which  people believe that they can produce  fancy computer models and you ask them  what determines inflation in your model  and they say the inflation target  and then you say but surely you can't  just announce  what inflation will be it reflects  what's going on in the economy and the  models don't take that into account  amazingly  so i i do think that there were this was  a mistake of the economics profession  which has spread  understandably into central banks which  employ the graduates produced by  by universities and they adopt the  conventional wisdom of the time  and i think it's misled us on this  occasion followed as i said by some bad  luck  is it um do you think this is a greater  challenge than than any that you faced  when you were governor but in some ways  i think it probably is though  i think the similarities that you went  through you know you had the financial  crisis yeah the financial crisis but i  think  in a way the the worse the situation  became the easier it was to know what  the answer was i mean i think in the  financial crisis  it was very  it didn't take very long to work out  that the banks were under capitalized  that the banks themselves had borrowed  too much and they were very fragile and  the only way to restore confidence in  the banking system was to recapitalize  the banks it took time to  persuade everyone to do it but  eventually we got there  now i think  again we know what the problem is  monetary policy has been too loose it  needs to be tightened  and the problem for central banks is  that  for very understandable reasons they  don't want to say you know  maybe we got it wrong in the last year  or so um  the you know the the obvious piece of  advice you give to a central bank today  is  i wouldn't start from here  but they are and yeah that's the problem  and  i mean there have been lots of there's  been lots of scrutiny shall we say of of  the bank of england  in the last few weeks  i mean that's there's going to be more  isn't there well there's bad to be more  when when inflation rises so far above  target  but i think the important thing for them  is to give very strong clear signals  that they will take action  to bring inflation down and they're  going to have to be patient  um just as we were in the financial  crisis the the first  six to 12 months of that were very  difficult for the bank we felt we were  doing the right thing and i think in  time we were proved right  you know i think provided central banks  take appropriate action now  they will see a fall in inflation next  year  and  no predictions but i think this will you  know the  very appropriate action meaning raising  interest rates quite considerably well i  i don't want to speculate on you know  how far they should do it but i think  making doing something which illustrates  that it's not just talking about it it's  its actions are they all raising rates  they're raising rates the trouble is  that present rates are so low relative  to where  they ought to be in order to have a  chance of bringing inflation back down  and  what i hope is that they just don't  leave it too long because then they may  end up raising rates  but at the wrong time you know  this is the time to do it  not in six months 12 months time when we  may be facing a much you know  more difficult economic situation um  put all of this together and  i mean how concerned are you about  bank of england independence this is a  story that you are you know you're a big  part of it you're one of the the you  know part of the origin story in a way  well i  i don't think there is a threat to  legal independence of the bank of  england i think any government that  tried to undo independence  would immediately see a very adverse  reaction in markets government the rate  at which governments could borrow but it  would go up  so i i don't think that's a threat i i  think  what is most important now is that the  bank has a very clear and simple  narrative i think  don't keep it too complicated keep it  simple  which makes it easier then for  governments to support and back  the central bank  do you worry though that you're by you  know intervening like this or making  these points as you are with your  reputation that that might then  give those who want to knock the bank  more ammunition to take them down well  let me be very clear i'm criticizing the  ideas of the economics profession  which have come to influence central  banks it's the ideas that are wrong and  i don't criticize individuals in it  because i think you can't blame them  first you know going along with what is  a conventional wisdom  what we need is a debate about the ideas  behind all this  and i think that's what's what's  important and if we can change that  narrative somewhat i think it would be  easier for the bank because as i said if  you look ahead they're not printing  money at a rapid rate now  so they have a good story to tell  about where inflation is going and why  it will come back to the target in due  course  but they've got to have that simple  narrative i think  in order to retain the credibility  which was built up  over a long period  since really in 1992 and a lot further  since independence in 1997.  25 years ago yeah 25 this is the  anniversary so it's funny how  i think various anniversaries there's  been a crisis that we've ended up  talking about more than the  successes of the uh of the past few  decades but if i look back at my time in  the bank of england  which i joined in 1991  i think there was a crisis every single  year in some part of the world which  affected what we were trying to achieve  uh but by the way are you passing are  you passing this advice on  you know to to colleagues at the bank  well i don't give advice  in public and whether or not i do it in  private  i will reflect on it i'm always there  they know how to get hold of me
 

